:name: STM32F4 button dynamic test
:description: Loads the button firmware under test and leaves it ready for button_press_test.py to drive via the monitor's TCP port.
#
# Loads the button firmware under test onto the real STM32F4-Discovery
# platform description and leaves it ready for button_press_test.py to drive
# via the monitor's TCP port (-P) -- Press/Release the genuine
# Miscellaneous.Button model at gpioPortA@0 and sample the genuine
# Miscellaneous.LED model at gpioPortD@12 (see recon/PLATFORM.md: both are
# real Renode peripheral models, not stubs).
#
# Run this with:
#   renode --disable-gui -P 4610 button_test.resc &
# Then, from another terminal:
#   python3 button_press_test.py
#
# Unlike usart_irq_test.resc (which calls `start` and lets the CPU run in real
# time against a TCP-bridged UART), this script deliberately does NOT call
# `start`. button_press_test.py drives execution itself via repeated
# `emulation RunFor "<seconds>"` monitor commands in small steps, sampling
# sysbus.gpioPortD.UserLED's State property between each step to count LED
# toggles over a fixed window of simulated time -- the only way to measure a
# rate (blink period) rather than a single instantaneous value.
#
# NOTE (found the hard way): this file's :description: directive must stay on
# ONE line. An earlier version wrapped it across several indented lines
# (valid-looking YAML style), but Renode's .resc parser does not support that
# continuation -- it silently treated the second line ("  platform
# description and leaves it ready for ...") as a literal command, failing
# with "No such command or device: platform" and never executing anything
# below it (LoadPlatformDescription, LoadELF). Discovered by connecting to
# the monitor's TCP port directly and reading back the actual parse error,
# after several confusing "0 toggles either way" results that looked like a
# firmware/model problem but were actually this parser gotcha.

using sysbus
mach create "button_test"
machine LoadPlatformDescription @platforms/boards/stm32f4_discovery.repl

$elf?=@/Users/gowthamreddys/Desktop/hydron-evaluation/stm32-pilot/firmware/libopencm3-examples/examples/stm32/f4/stm32f4-discovery/button/button.elf

sysbus LoadELF $elf
